course: dictionary
lesson: 11610
title: Formal words: appreciable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Appreciable is a formal word. It means big enough to be noticed or measured. If a change is appreciable, it is real and you can see or measure it, so it is not tiny. «There was an appreciable difference between the two test scores.» The difference was big enough to notice.
explain: Try the swap check from before. In «The plant grew an appreciable amount in one week», you could swap in «a noticeable amount», and the sentence still makes sense. The adverb is appreciably, as in «The temperature rose appreciably by noon.»
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Words like difference, amount and effect tell you that something can be measured. When a sentence says «no appreciable change», it means the change was too small to matter.
fact: Appreciable means large or important enough to be noticed or measured.
fact: Appreciable is a formal word, often used in science and exam texts.
fact: Common phrases are appreciable difference, appreciable amount and appreciable effect.
fact: «No appreciable change» means the change was too small to notice.
fact: Do not confuse appreciable with appreciate, which means to value something or to be thankful for it.
exercise: «The new fence made no appreciable difference to the noise.» What does appreciable mean in this sentence? | Very loud | *Big enough to be noticed or measured | Made of metal | Easy to climb over
exercise: Which word in «The rain brought an appreciable amount of water to the dry fields.» tells you the amount was big enough to matter? | *appreciable | fields | brought | rain
exercise: Ella says appreciable means thankful, like appreciate. What does it really mean? | Thankful for something kind | *Big enough to be noticed | Very small | Hidden under the water
test: «The medicine had an appreciable effect on the fever within an hour.» What does appreciable show here? | The effect was tiny and hard to see | *The effect was big enough to be noticed | The medicine tasted bitter | The fever lasted all day
test: Which phrase shows a difference that is real and easy to measure? | a barely there hint | a silent cough | a wet afternoon | *an appreciable difference
exam: «After the rain, the river rose appreciably overnight.» Which word means about the same as appreciably? | *noticeably, by a measurable amount | slowly, over many years | slightly, by a tiny amount | quietly, without anyone knowing
